            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19549634?u=386f7d3a43a7d4b6159b6bbf86a36c79c0586601&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><h3 dir=\"auto\">Summary</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"issue-keyword tooltipped tooltipped-se\" aria-label=\"This pull request closes issue #44640.\">Fixes</span> <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"4809089090\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/44640\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/issues/44640/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/44640\">#44640</a></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The Example section on the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;use&gt;</code> page says it draws \"a circle with a different fill and stroke color,\" and the last copy sets <code class=\"notranslate\">stroke=\"red\"</code>. But the referenced <code class=\"notranslate\">#myCircle</code> already set <code class=\"notranslate\">stroke=\"blue\"</code>, and a <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;use&gt;</code> copy cannot override a presentation attribute that the referenced element already defines, so the red was ignored and the circle rendered blue.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Change</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Remove <code class=\"notranslate\">stroke=\"blue\"</code> from the referenced circle so the <code class=\"notranslate\">stroke=\"red\"</code> on the last copy renders, matching what the surrounding text describes. The attribute-override behavior is still explained in the Usage notes section, so nothing is lost.</p>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/44696",
            "title": "fix(svg): render red stroke in <use> example",
            "date_modified": "2026-07-13T16:20:48.000Z",
            "date_published": "2026-07-12T23:57:39.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "kirthi-b",
                "url": "https://github.com/kirthi-b"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69888?v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>Capitalizing words in a URL enables screen readers to read words as words rather than a string of letters.</li>\n<li>generally we put the source outside the link, directly after it, in external links in the see also section</li>\n</ol>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/44694",
            "title": "See also link formatting on GlobalPrivacyControl",
            "date_modified": "2026-07-13T10:12:14.000Z",
            "date_published": "2026-07-12T17:44:16.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "estelle",
                "url": "https://github.com/estelle"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/91409429?u=4e1f7b62160e72ba743c04565340fd30f04e290a&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><p dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"issue-keyword tooltipped tooltipped-se\" aria-label=\"This pull request closes issue #44618.\">Fixes</span> <a href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/44618\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/issues/44618/hovercard\">mdn/content#44618</a></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The Description section of <code class=\"notranslate\">Temporal.ZonedDateTime.prototype.add()</code> claimed that offset-ambiguous results use <code class=\"notranslate\">offset: \"prefer\"</code>, but the spec's AddZonedDateTime algorithm uses <code class=\"notranslate\">disambiguation: \"compatible\"</code> — the same mechanism as the date-time validity bullet directly above.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Also fixes the second example: subtracting one day from <code class=\"notranslate\">2024-11-04T01:00:00-05:00[America/New_York]</code> yields <code class=\"notranslate\">2024-11-03T01:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]</code> (offset -04:00, not -05:00 as previously stated).</p>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/44693",
            "title": "Fix #44618: Temporal.ZonedDateTime.add() offset ambiguity example and description",
            "date_modified": "2026-07-12T19:52:14.000Z",
            "date_published": "2026-07-12T14:18:41.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "hanu-14",
                "url": "https://github.com/hanu-14"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/15889080?u=e9ac07efaae7e616e6623bb8edc993177f126661&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">This change defines that <code class=\"notranslate\">Element.slot</code> reflects the <code class=\"notranslate\">slot</code> global attribute, even if the element is not actually slotted.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">I was misled by the current definition, which led me to think that the property represents the name of the slot where the element is actually placed.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Additional details</h3>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Related issues and pull requests</h3>\n\n\n\n",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/44687",
            "title": "Clarify that Element.slot reflects the attribute",
